A signal \(x(t)\) is given by \[x(t)=
\begin{cases}
1, &amp; -\dfrac{T}{4}&lt;t\le \dfrac{3T}{4},\\ -1, &amp; \dfrac{3T}{4}&lt;t\le \dfrac{7T}{4},\\ -x(t+T), &amp; \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}\] Which among the following gives the fundamental Fourier term of \(x(t)\)?

questions.explanation:
The signal satisfies \[x(t+T)=-x(t),\] which means it has half-wave symmetry. Hence, the fundamental period is \[T_0=2T,\qquad \omega_0=\frac{2\pi}{T_0}=\frac{\pi}{T}.\] Over one period, \[x(t)=
\begin{cases}
1, &amp; -\dfrac{T}{4}&lt;t\le\dfrac{3T}{4},\\ -1, &amp; \dfrac{3T}{4}&lt;t\le\dfrac{7T}{4}.
\end{cases}\] The waveform is a 50% duty-cycle square wave centered at \[t=\frac{T}{4}.\] The fundamental component of a unit-amplitude square wave is \[\frac{4}{\pi}\cos\!\left[\omega_0\left(t-\frac{T}{4}\right)\right].\] Substituting \[\omega_0=\frac{\pi}{T},\] gives \[x_1(t)=\frac{4}{\pi}
\cos\!\left(\frac{\pi t}{T}-\frac{\pi}{4}\right).\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) }\displaystyle \frac{4}{\pi}\cos\!\left(\frac{\pi t}{T}-\frac{\pi}{4}\right)}\]

The given signal has the highest frequency \[f_{\max}=1\,\text{kHz}.\] The sampling interval is \[T_{s}=1\,\text{ms}.\] Hence, the sampling frequency is \[f_{s}=\frac{1}{T_{s}}
=\frac{1}{1\times10^{-3}}
=1\,\text{kHz}.\] The Nyquist rate is \[2f_{\max}=2\times1=2\,\text{kHz}.\] Since \[f_{s}&lt;2f_{\max},\] the Nyquist criterion is violated and aliasing occurs. The spectrum of the sampled signal is \[U_{T}(\omega)
=\frac{1}{T_{s}}
\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}
U(\omega-n\omega_{s}),\] where \[\omega_{s}=2\pi f_{s}.\] Because the replicas are spaced by only \(1\,\text{kHz}\), which is less than the Nyquist rate, they overlap completely, producing a constant (flat) spectrum due to aliasing. \[\boxed{\text{Option B}}\]

