=
-\frac{\Delta P}{P_R}\times\text{droop}\] where \(P_R=15\,\text{MW}\) is the maximum continuous power limit of the prime mover. Hence, \[\Delta f
=
-\frac{3.5}{15}\times0.05\times50\] \[\Delta f=-0.583\,\text{Hz}\] Therefore, the initially settled frequency would be \[f=50-0.583\] \[f=49.417\,\text{Hz}\] However, the total load is \[P_{\text{total}}=12+3.5=15.5\,\text{MW},\] which exceeds the maximum continuous power limit of \(15\,\text{MW}\). Hence, the prime mover cannot sustain the load and the rotor decelerates. Consequently, the frequency continues to decrease until it falls below \(48\,\text{Hz}\), causing one of the \(4\,\text{MW}\) loads to trip. The remaining load is, therefore, \[P_L=15.5-4=11.5\,\text{MW}.\] The change in load from the initial \(12\,\text{MW}\) is \[\Delta P=11.5-12=-0.5\,\text{MW}.\] Hence, \[\frac{\Delta f}{50}
=
-\frac{-0.5}{15}\times0.05\] \[\Delta f=0.083\,\text{Hz}\] Thus, the final frequency is \[f=50+0.083\] \[\boxed{C}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Governor droop characteristic: \[\frac{\Delta f}{f}
=
-\frac{\Delta P}{P_{\text{rated}}}
\times \text{droop}\]</li><li>An increase in load causes a reduction in frequency.</li><li>Under-frequency relays disconnect loads when frequency falls below the preset threshold.</li><li>Rotor decelerates when electrical power exceeds mechanical input power.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not neglect the negative sign in the droop equation.</li><li>Do not stop at \(49.417\,\text{Hz}\); check whether the load exceeds the prime-mover limit.</li><li>Remember that under-frequency load shedding changes the final connected load.</li></ul></div>

