Two large power systems are interconnected through <ul><li>an AC transmission line, and</li><li>an HVDC link.</li></ul> The HVDC link transfers a controllable power \[P_{dc}\] while the AC tie-line transfers \[P_{ac}.\] <strong>Analysis of Option (A)</strong><br/>Since the two regions are connected through an AC transmission line, they operate synchronously in steady state. Therefore, \[f_1=f_2\] and both regions must have the same frequency. Hence, Option (A) is incorrect. <strong>Analysis of Option (B)</strong><br/>Changing the firing angle of the converter can directly control \[P_{dc}.\] However, changing \(P_{dc}\) modifies the power-angle relationship of the interconnected AC system, causing the AC tie-line power \[P_{ac}\] to readjust automatically. Therefore, HVDC controls do not independently determine the total interchange \[(P_{ac}+P_{dc}).\] The total power transfer is governed by generation-load balance and system operating conditions. Hence, Option (B) is incorrect. <strong>Analysis of Option (C)</strong><br/>The HVDC link provides fast and independent control of DC power transfer. By increasing or decreasing \[P_{dc},\] the AC tie-line power adjusts correspondingly. Thus, the proportion of power carried by the AC line and the HVDC link can be changed without modifying the AC network. For example, \[P_{\text{total}}
=
P_{ac}+P_{dc}\] may remain nearly constant while the individual values of \[P_{ac}
\quad \text{and} \quad
P_{dc}\] change. Therefore, the power-sharing between the AC line and the HVDC link can be controlled through HVDC converter controls alone. Hence, Option (C) is correct. <strong>Analysis of Option (D)</strong><br/>Modern HVDC systems allow reversal of power flow by changing converter operating conditions. Thus, \[P_{dc}\] can be reversed whenever required. Hence, Option (D) is incorrect. Therefore, the correct answer is \[\boxed{\text{(C) Power sharing between the AC line and HVDC link can be changed by controlling the HVDC converters alone}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>AC-connected regions must operate at the same steady-state frequency.</li><li>HVDC control can redistribute power flow between parallel AC and DC corridors.</li><li>One major advantage of HVDC links is controllable power transfer independent of AC line impedance.</li><li>Power reversal in HVDC systems is possible through converter control.</li></ul></div>

